A Southern classic made famous by Ree Drummond (The Pioneer Woman). Double-breaded tender cube steaks fried until shatteringly crisp and served alongside creamy mashed potatoes under a blanket of scratch skillet pepper gravy.

*Adapted from [Ree Drummond / Food Network](http://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/ree-drummond/chicken-fried-steak-with-gravy-recipe).*

## Ingredients

### Steaks & Breading
- 3 pounds cube steak (extra-tenderized round steak)
- Kosher salt and freshly ground black pepper, to taste
- 1 ½ cups whole milk
- 2 large eggs
- 2 cups all-purpose flour
- 2 teaspoons seasoned salt (such as Lawry's)
- 1 ½ teaspoons freshly ground black pepper
- ¾ teaspoon paprika
- ¼ teaspoon cayenne pepper
- ½ cup canola or vegetable oil
- 1 tablespoon butter

### Skillet Pepper Gravy
- ⅓ cup all-purpose flour
- 3 to 4 cups whole milk
- ½ teaspoon seasoned salt
- Freshly ground black pepper, to taste

### For Serving
- Warm homemade mashed potatoes

## Instructions

1. **Set Up the Breading Station:** Arrange three wide, shallow dishes in an assembly line:
   - Dish 1: Whisk together 1 ½ cups whole milk and 2 eggs.
   - Dish 2: Whisk together 2 cups flour, 2 teaspoons seasoned salt, 1 ½ teaspoons black pepper, paprika, and cayenne pepper.
   - Dish 3: The seasoned cube steaks.
   - Have a clean rimmed sheet or plate at the end of the line ready for breaded meat.
2. **Double-Dredge the Steaks:** Working with one piece of meat at a time, lightly sprinkle both sides with kosher salt and black pepper. Dredge in the flour mixture to coat, dip into the milk/egg mixture, and return to the flour mixture for a second coating (dry / wet / dry). Place the breaded steak on the clean plate and repeat with remaining steaks.
3. **Fry the Steaks:** Heat ½ cup oil and 1 tablespoon butter in a large cast-iron or heavy skillet over medium heat. Test the heat with a pinch of flour; when it sizzles immediately without burning, it is ready. Fry steaks in batches (about 3 pieces at a time) for about 2 minutes per side until the edges turn golden and crisp. Transfer to a paper towel-lined plate and tent loosely with foil to keep warm.
4. **Make the Skillet Roux:** Pour off the excess grease from the skillet into a heatproof bowl. Return the skillet (with all its flavorful browned bits) to the stove over medium-low heat. Add ¼ cup of the hot grease back into the skillet. Sprinkle ⅓ cup flour evenly across the grease and whisk continuously, cooking for 1 to 2 minutes until a deep golden-brown paste forms.
5. **Simmer the Cream Gravy:** Slowly stream in 3 to 4 cups of milk while whisking constantly to prevent lumps. Whisk in ½ teaspoon seasoned salt and plenty of freshly ground black pepper. Simmer gently for 5 to 10 minutes, whisking frequently, until the gravy thickens into a rich, luscious sauce (add a splash more milk if it becomes too thick).
6. **Assemble and Serve:** Plate the crispy steaks with generous mounds of mashed potatoes, then ladle piping hot skillet pepper gravy over everything.

## Notes

- **The Double Dredge:** The dry-wet-dry dipping technique gives cube steak its signature craggy, thick crust that clings to the meat rather than falling off in the pan.
- **Skillet Flavor:** Never wipe out the pan before making the gravy; those browned bits left from frying the steaks are where all the rich flavor lives.
